{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791195288,"nonce":"9oZoMZrV+c+cKp5OQDZRX8UajBE=","title":"Measurement of a weight the renderer has not loaded yet falls back to the estimator","message":"FontTextMeasurer resolves its face with get_for_render_with_style, which only\nreads the registry's cache and keys on the EXACT weight. A face that has not\nbeen loaded for that weight returns None, and measurement silently uses\nestimate_size: 0.55 times the font size per character.\n\nObserved, once, with a fresh registry in a throwaway test (removed). At 32px\nfor \"The Rich Text Editor\", with System resolving to SF Pro:\n\n    weight 400  measured 264.0   real face 264.0   (face was cached)\n    weight 700  measured 352.0   real face 286.6   (face was not)\n\n352.0 is exactly 20 characters times 32 times 0.55: the estimator's formula.\n\nWhy it matters: the renderer loads the bold face when it first draws, so the\nfirst layout of a bold run is sized by the estimator and any later layout by\nthe real face. Boxes can change size once the face arrives, or never if\nnothing triggers a relayout.\n\nNOT verified in a running app: how often a face is genuinely uncached at first\nmeasure there, because the generic families are preloaded at init and weights\nother than the one preloaded may be the only gap. The numbers above come from a\nfresh registry, which is the worst case.\n\nSame shape as fallback faces, which are now resolved (and loaded when the text\nneeds them) before measuring: 124ad5d. A fix would load the requested weight on\na miss, as that does. The reason it does not today is documented in\ntext_measurer.rs: preloading could trigger a system font scan during startup.\nThe tradeoff is first-layout accuracy against startup cost.","files":null}]}